Fix two ways IOConnection's send timer loses real time.

Found by timing class 1 traffic at the interface rather than trusting the
stack's own view of what it sent - see docs/eip-rpi-evaluation.md.

1. notifyTick() truncated elapsed time to whole milliseconds and then set
   _lastHandleTime from the untruncated clock, so the sub-millisecond
   remainder was discarded permanently rather than carried forward. That value
   drives the connection timeout accounting as well as the send timer, so the
   configured (4 << multiplier) x RPI was not the timeout in effect.

2. On sending, _o2tTimer was zeroed rather than decremented by the period.
   The tick that crosses the deadline almost never lands exactly on it, so
   zeroing discards the overshoot and the mean period becomes _o2tAPI plus the
   mean overshoot. This was the dominant error: at a 10 ms RPI the measured
   O->T period was 11982 us; fixing only (1) left it at 12042 us; fixing (2)
   brought it to 10002 us, delivering 99.98% of the expected frames.

diff --git a/src/IOConnection.cpp b/src/IOConnection.cpp
index 5571496..065a39a 100644
--- a/src/IOConnection.cpp
+++ b/src/IOConnection.cpp
@@ -94,9 +94,9 @@ namespace eipScanner {
 	bool IOConnection::notifyTick() {
 		auto now = std::chrono::steady_clock::now();
 		auto sinceLastHandle =
-			std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(now - _lastHandleTime);
+			std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::microseconds>(now - _lastHandleTime);
 
-		auto periodInMicroS = sinceLastHandle.count() * 1000;
+		auto periodInMicroS = sinceLastHandle.count();
 		_connectionTimeoutCount += periodInMicroS;
 		if (_connectionTimeoutCount > _connectionTimeoutMultiplier * _t2oAPI) {
 			Logger(LogLevel::WARNING) << "Connection SeriaNumber=" << _serialNumber << " is closed by timeout";
@@ -124,7 +124,11 @@ namespace eipScanner {
 				buffer << header;
 			}
 
-			_o2tTimer = 0;
+			/* Subtract the period rather than zeroing: the tick that crosses the
+			 * deadline almost never lands exactly on it, and discarding the
+			 * overshoot restarts the next period early of where it should, so
+			 * the mean period becomes _o2tAPI plus the mean overshoot. */
+			_o2tTimer -= _o2tAPI;
 			_sendDataHandle(_outputData);
 			if (_o2tFixedSize && _outputData.size() != _o2tDataSize)  {
 				Logger(LogLevel::WARNING) << "Connection O2T_ID=" << _o2tNetworkConnectionId
